{"team":"LV","col":"#C9D2DC","identity":"balanced","funnel":"balanced-D","plays_pg":58.7,"adot":6.7,"report_html":"<div class=\"report\"><h2>A Balanced Attack</h2><p><strong>Brock Bowers</strong> is a genuine centerpiece, not an afterthought \u2014 <strong>22%</strong> of targets (league ~15%).</p><h3>The Offensive Blueprint</h3><p>They run downhill \u2014 mostly inside the tackles (16% to the edge vs ~25%), a gap/power scheme that trades big plays for a steady, grinding floor.</p><p>When they fall behind they abandon the run entirely \u2014 61% pass while trailing, so their pass-catchers have huge comeback-script ceilings.</p><p>By the numbers it's a struggling unit \u2014 a 39% success rate (below the 44% average).</p><p>It's a short, rhythm-based passing game \u2014 aDOT just <strong>6.7</strong> \u2014 that lives on YAC and checkdowns, boosting reception totals over big-play yardage.</p><h3>In the Red Zone</h3><p>Inside the 20 they stay aggressive, throwing <strong>59%</strong> of the time, which puts scoring equity on <strong>Tre Tucker</strong>. And with the TE commanding 22% of targets, <strong>Brock Bowers</strong> is a live red-zone option too.</p><h2>Defense \u2014 A Balanced Defense</h2><p>They surrender <strong>118 rushing</strong> and <strong>224 passing</strong> yards per game (league ~120 / ~230). No dominant funnel \u2014 read it matchup by matchup.</p><p>They leak <strong>TE touchdowns</strong> (0.32/game vs ~0.24) \u2014 an anytime-TD spot to attack.</p><p>They leak <strong>RB rushing TDs</strong> (0.53/game vs ~0.46) \u2014 an anytime-TD spot to attack.</p><p>They leak <strong>WR1 receiving TDs</strong> (0.44/game vs ~0.38) \u2014 an anytime-TD spot to attack.</p><h2>The Betting Angles</h2><p>Bottom line: their pass-catchers have massive ceilings in comeback scripts (fade-material as underdogs); <strong>opposing TE anytime-TD</strong> is a repeatable angle.</p><p><em>Built on 2024\u201325 data; the profile rebuilds through the season as the curren